--TEST--
`Core\IO\FileMode`'s four cases are the whole of what replaces `fopen`'s mode-string grammar, and each does exactly one thing to the file that is already there: `Append` keeps it, `Write` empties it, `ReadWrite` creates it and empties nothing — spec § 14 R11
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\IO\FileMode
// PHP writes this as `"a"`, `"w"`, `"c+"` and nine other spellings whose
// differences a caller has to remember; here it is an enum, so a mode that is
// not one of these four is refused before the program runs and there is no
// `b`/`t` axis at all -- Novis text is octets, so every mode is what PHP would
// call binary.
Core\IO\File $a = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Append);
uint $wrote = $a->write("second\n");
echo "appended ", $wrote as string, " bytes\n";
$a->close();

Core\IO\File $r1 = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
echo $r1->read(64);
$r1->close();

// `Write` is the destructive one, and it is destructive on open rather than on
// the first write: the file is empty from the moment the handle exists.
Core\IO\File $w = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
Core\IO\File $peek = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
echo "emptied on open: ", Core\Str::length($peek->read(64)) as string, "\n";
$peek->close();
$w->write("third\n");
$w->close();

Core\IO\File $r2 = Core\IO::open("note.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
echo $r2->read(64);
$r2->close();

// `ReadWrite` is `fopen`'s `c+` and not its `r+` or `w+`: it creates a path
// that is not there, which `r+` refuses, and it keeps what is, which `w+`
// throws away. Those two surprises are the reason the other `+` forms are not
// cases of this enum.
Core\IO\File $made = Core\IO::open("fresh.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
$made->write("made\n");
$made->close();

Core\IO\File $again = Core\IO::open("fresh.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
echo "kept on reopen: ", $again->read(64);
$again->close();
--FILE note.txt--
first
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
appended 7 bytes
first
second
emptied on open: 0
third
kept on reopen: made
